@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# E3 in Isaac Sim: closed-loop PhysX extension
+
+This backend runs the seeded E3 scenarios using the real robot articulation and measured physics feedback. It is a
+separate experiment from the ideal-unicycle 2D benchmark. Do not combine the two result sets or call robot-trajectory
+playback a physical navigation experiment.
+
+## Experiment grid
+
+Use a frozen selection manifest in the format written by `e3_select_scenes.py`. Ten selected layouts, three tiers,
+ten paired seeds, and three policies define 900 planned trials. Base variants remove authored agents; hard variants
+add 2–4 shelves and retain the E3 static and aisle checks. Invalid variants are recorded rather than replaced after
+navigation outcomes. `prepare` must complete the entire selection before native trials can start.
+
+```powershell
+uv run --extra mesh python scripts/e3_isaac_suite.py prepare results/e3_scene_selection/selection.json --seeds 10 --out out/e3_isaac_v1
+uv run --extra mesh python scripts/e3_isaac_suite.py run out/e3_isaac_v1 --isaac-python C:/isaacsim/python.exe
+```
+
+On a Linux standalone installation, pass its `python.sh`. A pip installation must use that installation's Python
+executable. Host planning dependencies and standalone `usd-core` must never be added to Kit's Python environment.
+
+The default minimum Isaac version is 5.0, matching worldc's original runtime gate. An existing Isaac 4.5 installation
+can be explicitly selected with `--allow-isaac-4.5`; its engine version is recorded and its results must remain separate.
+This does not change the E1 runtime gate or prove that 5.1 scene assets load correctly in 4.5. A real articulation,
+successful wheel calibration, valid measured state, and contact delivery are required for every trial. There is no
+proxy-body, kinematic-robot, missing-asset, or 2D fallback in the physical worker.
+
+## Controller and physics
+
+- The host runs a feedback port of `e3-policies-v1` at 20 Hz. The original frozen simulator and robot table remain
+  unchanged. Ideal-plant parity tests compare the feedback port with the frozen reference, including turning,
+  protective fields, human-aware replanning, rear approaches, and freezing.
+- Kit steps physics at 120 Hz. Only wheel velocity targets move the robot during a trial. Its position and velocity
+  come from the articulation, not numerical integration in the host.
+- Wheel gains and travel heading are calibrated in physics. The articulation's full state is reset before episode
+  time zero. Human tracks do not advance during calibration.
+- Scripted humans use kinematic capsules with radius 0.26 m and height 1.775 m. The same seeded tracks are shared
+  across policies and interpolated onto physics steps. They do not react to the robot or each other.
+- Observations are perfect simulator state. This evaluates control and physical interaction, not perception accuracy.
+- Tilt above 20 degrees or falling below the floor ends the trial as `physics_failure`. This is an additional physical
+  failure category absent from the ideal 2D backend. Contact-recorder errors are measurement failures and stop the run.
+
+## Metrics
+
+The original E3 footprint-to-human-disk metrics are computed from measured state at the same 20 Hz control clock.
+Turning counts as moving. These retain the 2D experiment's geometric definitions for comparison, but are labeled
+`E3 geometric` in the physical result table.
+
+Separately, the native callback records PhysX contact episodes. A report is confirmed only when a contact impulse
+exceeds 1e-5 N.s. Sustained contacts are counted once until separation, and self-contacts and floor contacts are
+excluded. Native episodes are per collider pair, not unique human hazard incidents. They include human and static
+obstacle contacts and must not be equated numerically with the per-human geometric episode count.
+
+`physx_success` means all goals reached with no contact episode beginning while the robot moves. `physx_contact_free_success`
+requires completion with no confirmed contact episodes of either motion class. The original `success` remains E3's
+geometric moving-contact definition. Both kinds of success are reported. Robot motion classification is not causal
+responsibility. No result constitutes standards compliance or real-world safety certification.
+
+## Required gates and evidence
+
+Before navigation, an isolated native diagnostic must show floor-only control, two force-bearing wall-contact
+episodes, sustained-contact deduplication, and separation after retreat. A failed diagnostic blocks the campaign.
+It is not counted as a benchmark trial. Every trial also requires floor callback delivery and zero callback errors.
+
+The campaign records scene/track/code hashes, policy and engine versions, native logs, measured trajectories,
+120 Hz robot traces, contact events, and outcomes. A broken bridge or infrastructure failure stops further native
+attempts; it is not converted into a collision-free result.
+
+After complete trials, hard/seed-0/blind is independently rerun for every selected scene. Physical replay tolerances are fixed before outcomes:
+maximum XY error 0.01 m, heading error 0.02 rad, duration error 0.1 s, and matching outcome, goal count, geometric moving
+contacts, and native episode count. This is a physical repeatability check, not the 2D bit-identical replay test.
+Analysis runs only after the replay passes. `e3_isaac_analysis.py` adds native contact and physical-failure columns to
+the existing paired-bootstrap analysis, and rejects mixed 2D/Isaac or mixed-engine input.
+
+## Checkpoints and geometry configuration
+
+`prepare --transform-seed N` fixes the geometry draw separately from the ten trial seeds. The default remains 0.
+The selected seed and every shelf pose are recorded; all policies and trial seeds use the same hard geometry.
+Any nondefault seed must be reported when comparing against the default 2D experiment. It does not change the
+allowed 2–4 shelves, 1.2 m aisle, static checks, worker counts, or frozen controller definitions.
+
+Every completed trial checkpoints its result and evidence hashes and updates `trials.csv`. After interruption,
+`run ... --resume` verifies unchanged code, prepared scenes and tracks, engine version, results, and native evidence before reusing
+completed trials. Incomplete attempts remain in place; restarted attempts use new directories. Infrastructure failures
+stop the batch and require diagnosis; resume does not silently replace failed measurements. An OS file lock prevents
+two Isaac campaigns from this project running together. The manifest becomes `complete` only after all physical
+trials, one replay per scene, and analysis pass.
+
+The native runner holds layout bodies fixed, disables inherited visual physics, and enables contact reporting on
+every robot link after removing stage instancing. Each trial writes `native_setup.json` confirming that inherited
+visual colliders are disabled. Workers receive initial transforms before the first measured step;
+later motion uses kinematic targets. PhysX advances at an explicit 1/120 s and its callback clock is checked each step.
+Contact patches for the same collider pair are merged within a callback to avoid treating a lost patch plus a
+persisting patch as a new episode. Raw episodes are not unique human incidents.
+
+## Validation scope
+
+Host parity and orchestration tests do not establish native wheel dynamics, asset compatibility, or callback delivery.
+Those require the actual native probe and a completed physical pilot before a full 900-trial campaign. The local
+Isaac 4.5 contact probe is an instrumentation diagnostic only; failed attempts remain separate from successful ones.
